  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14165497/assets/eda2132e8e050399298b97dc9d36cf27862d6aa4625e7aa870bb4c8fd246e781.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of triangles to the number of pentagons?",
  "worked_solution": "We count the number of triangles. We see that there are $2$ triangles.\n\nWe count the number of pentagons. We see that there are $4$ pentagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $2:4.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $1:2.$",
